+# OpenList control-plane architecture specification
+
+## Status
+
+This document defines the target architecture for the repository currently named
+`OpenList`. It is a design contract and migration plan. It does not claim that
+the control-plane/data-plane split has already landed.
+
+The canonical application role is:
+
+```text
+openlist-control-plane
+```
+
+The corresponding execution application is the separately owned
+`network-data-plane-worker`.
+
+## Contract ownership
+
+The data-plane worker owns the plugin contract.
+
+The canonical definitions of plugin descriptors, configuration-schema fields,
+secret declarations, typed plugin ports, lifecycle, capability manifests,
+generation replacement, and observed state belong to:
+
+```text
+wignerStan/pingora-worker/docs/network-data-plane-worker-spec.md
+```
+
+OpenList consumes those contracts. It does not define or fork them.
+
+OpenList may:
+
+- register and approve worker build digests;
+- cache an exact worker-published plugin descriptor by digest;
+- render administration forms from that descriptor;
+- structurally validate desired values against it;
+- store public configuration and secret references;
+- assign desired instances to compatible workers;
+- reconcile desired and observed generations.
+
+OpenList must not:
+
+- author a competing data-plane plugin schema;
+- hardcode provider fields as the canonical runtime contract;
+- add a capability absent from the worker descriptor;
+- reinterpret a field's secrecy or type;
+- construct a live provider client merely to render or validate a form;
+- send executable code through desired-state configuration.
+
+A concrete plugin implementation owns its concrete plugin ID, adapter IDs,
+configuration field declarations, secret field declarations, capabilities, and
+provider behavior by publishing a descriptor that conforms to the worker-owned
+contract.
+
+## Decision summary
+
+1. OpenList becomes the desired-state control plane for client instances,
+   namespaces, access policy, worker assignments, rollouts, and status.
+2. Object bytes never pass through the control plane.
+3. The control plane is not synchronously consulted by GET, PUT, LIST, COPY,
+   multipart, WebDAV, S3, or ordinary provider operations.
+4. Worker-reported plugin descriptors are authoritative for runtime capability
+   and configuration shape.
+5. OpenList stores and approves exact descriptor/build digests; it does not
+   manufacture plugin descriptors.
+6. Current OpenList driver implementations are migration input for a concrete
+   worker client plugin, but that plugin consumes the worker contract.
+7. Other client implementations are managed through the same generic desired
+   model. The control plane does not gain ownership of their schemas or runtime
+   behavior.
+8. There is no provider sidecar introduced merely to separate the planes, and no
+   OpenList-specific per-request provider RPC.
+9. Runtime configuration can select only capabilities reported by the assigned
+   worker from operator-installed, hash-approved native `.so` plugins. Provider
+   implementations are not linked into the Worker executable. Desired state
+   cannot install executable code.
+10. Current OpenList data-serving behavior remains only as a temporary legacy
+    migration profile. The final control-plane binary serves administration,
+    identity, configuration, reconciliation, and status—not file data.
+
+## Current implementation baseline
+
+The existing code combines desired configuration and runtime execution:
+
+- `model.Storage` stores mount-path identity, driver name, provider `Addition`
+  JSON, runtime status, enablement, sort/cache, and proxy behavior in one record;
+- storage creation writes the record, constructs the selected driver, unmarshals
+  its addition, initializes it, and inserts it into a process-global mount map;
+- update drops and reinitializes the live driver in the administrator request;
+- disable and delete drop live adapters while mutating the desired record;
+- driver field descriptions are derived from `Addition` struct tags;
+- the same process exposes administrator APIs and file data APIs, including
+  filesystem operations, downloads, WebDAV, and S3;
+- `drivers/all.go` blank-imports the complete adapter inventory.
+
+These mechanisms are useful migration inputs, but they are not the final plane
+boundary.
+
+## Target topology
+
+```text
+                             ADMINISTRATIVE TRAFFIC
+                                      |
+                                      v
++------------------------------------------------------------------------+
+|                      openlist-control-plane                            |
+|                                                                        |
+|  Admin UI / API            Identity / RBAC          Audit              |
+|  Descriptor registry       Client desired state     Secret vault       |
+|  Namespace / route model   Access credentials       Policy compiler    |
+|  Worker registry           Assignment compiler      Rollout/reconcile  |
+|  Desired revisions         Observed-state store     Status / events    |
++--------------------------------+---------------------------------------+
+                                 |
+               exact descriptor refs + desired snapshots + ACK
+               no object body, no per-request route/provider RPC
+                                 |
+                  +--------------+--------------+
+                  |                             |
+                  v                             v
++--------------------------------+  +--------------------------------+
+| network-data-plane-worker      |  | network-data-plane-worker      |
+| selected build A               |  | selected build B               |
+|                                |  |                                |
+| worker-owned plugin contract   |  | worker-owned plugin contract   |
+| loaded plugin descriptors      |  | loaded plugin descriptors      |
+| pinned runtime generations     |  | pinned runtime generations     |
++---------------+----------------+  +----------------+---------------+
+                |                                    |
+                v                                    v
+      External storage/provider APIs and other selected data services
+```
+
+A worker keeps its last-known-good applied generation while the control plane is
+unavailable. Control-plane reachability is never a condition for continuing an
+already admitted transfer.
+
+# Ownership boundary
+
+## Control plane owns
+
+- administrator and operator identity;
+- tenants or projects when multi-tenancy is enabled;
+- approved worker build digests;
+- approved plugin descriptor digests and policy around their use;
+- client desired state and revisions;
+- encrypted secret versions and rotation history;
+- namespace, bucket, host, path, and protocol-route desired state;
+- access credentials and policy bindings;
+- worker groups, selectors, and assignments;
+- snapshot compilation, signing, and delivery;
+- rollout intent, reconciliation, status aggregation, and audit.
+
+## Data-plane worker owns
+
+- the plugin descriptor and configuration-schema contract;
+- plugin registration and exact typed-port validation;
+- listeners and L4/L7 mechanics;
+- protocol parsing and request authentication;
+- local policy evaluation;
+- plugin and client generation construction and pinning;
+- provider sessions and access-token refresh;
+- streaming, Range, backpressure, cancellation, and retries;
+- multipart runtime state;
+- actual generation drain and physical cleanup;
+- request completion and operational telemetry.
+
+## Concrete plugin implementation owns
+
+- its stable plugin ID and version;
+- its implementation or adapter IDs;
+- concrete configuration field declarations;
+- concrete secret field declarations;
+- concrete capability declarations;
+- final typed configuration validation;
+- provider authentication and session behavior;
+- provider-specific object identity, upload, retry, direct-link, token-refresh,
+  and metadata behavior;
+- authoritative runtime results and cleanup.
+
+The plugin implementation may reuse OpenList provider-driver source, rclone
+packages, or another selected library. That implementation choice does not move
+its descriptor contract into the control plane or infra.
+
+## Infra owns
+
+Only reusable mechanisms, including:
+
+- Rust and Go L4/L7 facilities;
+- managed resource and plugin lifecycle;
+- native plugin loading;
+- action publication and dispatch;
+- local-control and selected remote carriers;
+- bounded storage/configuration primitives;
+- generic cryptographic and observability mechanisms.
+
+Infra must not import product-specific client implementations.
+
+# Descriptor consumption model
+
+## Authoritative source
+
+A worker binary publishes a capability manifest containing exact plugin IDs,
+contract versions, plugin versions, and descriptor digests. The worker makes the
+corresponding immutable descriptors available through its selected registration
+or control mechanism.
+
+The control plane keys a descriptor by at least:
+
+```text
+worker build digest
+plugin ID
+plugin version
+contract version
+descriptor digest
+```
+
+The descriptor bytes reported by an approved worker build are authoritative.
+OpenList may cache them, but it cannot edit them in place.
+
+## Registration flow
+
+```text
+worker starts
+    -> verifies installed .so artifact hashes before dynamic loading
+    -> validates exact plugin descriptors through the Worker contract
+    -> reports build and capability manifest
+control plane authenticates worker incarnation
+    -> verifies approved build and contract versions
+    -> fetches or receives exact descriptors
+    -> validates digest and bounds
+    -> records descriptor approval/availability
+```
+
+A worker name, deployment label, or language is not capability evidence.
+
+## Administration form flow
+
+```text
+administrator selects an available plugin implementation
+    -> OpenList loads the approved cached descriptor
+    -> UI renders bounded escaped field metadata
+    -> secret fields are write-only
+    -> OpenList performs structural validation
+    -> public values and secret values are stored separately
+```
+
+OpenList does not import a provider package or initialize a provider session to
+render this form.
+
+## Final validation flow
+
+Structural control-plane validation is not runtime acceptance.
+
+```text
+control plane commits desired client revision
+    -> snapshot references exact descriptor digest
+worker receives snapshot
+    -> verifies descriptor digest exists in its build
+    -> resolves sealed secret fields declared by that descriptor
+    -> plugin constructor performs final typed validation
+    -> candidate initializes
+    -> worker publishes generation or reports failure
+```
+
+A failed candidate leaves the current generation active.
+
+# Core terminology
+
+## Plugin descriptor
+
+The immutable description of one dynamically loaded plugin version, published by
+the concrete plugin under the Worker-owned contract. It
+contains its contract, implementation IDs, config schema, secret declarations,
+and capabilities.
+
+## Plugin approval
+
+A control-plane policy record authorizing an exact plugin/build/descriptor digest
+for selected worker groups or projects. Approval does not redefine the descriptor.
+
+## Client instance
+
+Desired configuration for one client implementation and account/session. One
+client may be referenced by multiple routes with different roots and policies.
+
+## Route
+
+An application route mapping a bucket, host, path, prefix, or namespace to a
+client instance and provider-relative root.
+
+## Configuration revision
+
+A control-plane revision for public desired values. It is distinct from the
+secret version, route revision, snapshot revision, worker incarnation, descriptor
+digest, and runtime generation.
+
+## Runtime generation
+
+The concrete worker-owned instance pinned by admitted work. The control plane can
+request replacement and observe it; the worker owns actual publication and drain.
+
+# Desired-state model
+
+Desired and observed state use separate records. Runtime status is never written
+back into the same row that defines operator intent.
+
+## Worker build approval
+
+```text
+build_digest
+implementation
+release/version
+approval_state
+allowed_worker_groups
+created_at
+updated_at
+```
+
+## Plugin descriptor record
+
+```text
+build_digest
+plugin_id
+plugin_version
+contract_version
+descriptor_digest
+descriptor_bytes_or_artifact_ref
+approval_state
+first_seen_at
+last_seen_at
+```
+
+The cached descriptor is immutable for its digest.
+
+## Client instance
+
+```text
+id
+project_id
+name
+plugin_id
+plugin_version
+implementation_id
+descriptor_digest
+config_revision
+public_config_json
+secret_set_id
+desired_enabled
+labels
+created_at
+updated_at
+deleted_at / tombstone_revision
+```
+
+The record never contains plaintext refresh tokens, cookies, passwords, or client
+secrets.
+
+## Secret set and version
+
+```text
+secret_set_id
+secret_version
+ciphertext / external_vault_reference
+key_id
+created_at
+revoked_at
+supersedes_version
+```
+
+Each secret name must be declared by the pinned descriptor.
+
+## Route
+
+```text
+id
+project_id
+lane
+protocol
+listener_or_route_key
+host / bucket / prefix
+client_id
+client_root
+policy_id
+credential_set_id
+desired_enabled
+route_revision
+labels
+```
+
+`client_root` is implementation-relative. It is not an OpenList mount-path
+identity.
+
+## Access credential
+
+```text
+id
+kind
+public_identifier
+secret_set_id
+policy_id
+desired_enabled
+revision
+```
+
+## Policy
+
+```text
+id
+version
+actions
+route selectors
+object-prefix selectors
+limits / constraints
+compiled_digest
+```
+
+## Worker
+
+```text
+worker_id
+worker_incarnation
+worker_group
+implementation
+build_digest
+capability_manifest_digest
+last_heartbeat
+readiness
+```
+
+## Assignment
+
+```text
+worker_group / selector
+resource set
+assignment_revision
+rollout strategy
+```
+
+## Desired snapshot
+
+```text
+snapshot_id
+worker_group
+snapshot_revision
+source_revision
+payload_digest
+signature
+created_at
+```
+
+## Observed plugin/client state
+
+```text
+worker_id
+worker_incarnation
+resource_id
+plugin_id
+descriptor_digest
+config_revision
+secret_version
+runtime_generation
+state
+last_error_code
+sanitized_message
+last_transition
+```
+
+## Worker applied state
+
+```text
+worker_id
+worker_incarnation
+snapshot_revision
+applied_digest
+state
+acknowledged_at
+resource_failures
+```
+
+## Audit event
+
+```text
+actor
+operation
+resource_type
+resource_id
+old_revision
+new_revision
+request_id
+timestamp
+sanitized_summary
+```
+
+# Mapping from the current `Storage` model
+
+The legacy row is split as follows:
+
+| Legacy field | Target owner |
+| --- | --- |
+| `ID` | client instance ID, with migration mapping |
+| `Driver` | desired plugin/implementation selection derived during migration |
+| `Addition` | public config plus encrypted/versioned secret references |
+| `MountPath` | route namespace and client-root mapping, not client identity |
+| `Disabled` | `desired_enabled=false` |
+| `Status` | observed runtime state, never desired state |
+| cache fields | route/application cache policy |
+| sort/index fields | UI/protocol route policy where still required |
+| proxy fields | L7 route or transport policy |
+| `Remark` | labels/description; legacy reference syntax is not a hidden contract |
+
+During migration, legacy driver `Addition` tags may be used to generate an initial
+plugin descriptor implementation. Once published, the worker-owned descriptor and
+its digest become authoritative. The control plane must not continue deriving a
+parallel schema independently from Go reflection.
+
+A deterministic mapping from old storage IDs and mount paths is retained so
+bookmarks, routes, and administrative workflows can be translated deliberately.
+
+# Control-plane services
+
+## Administrative API and UI
+
+The existing administrator UI is gradually remapped to:
+
+```text
+worker builds and plugin descriptors
+clients
+routes and namespaces
+credentials and secrets
+policies
+workers and assignments
+rollouts
+observed status and audit
+```
+
+The UI displays desired and observed state separately:
+
+- **Saved** means desired state was committed.
+- **Assigned** means a snapshot containing it was issued.
+- **Applied** means a worker published the requested generation.
+- **Drained** means the superseded generation physically finished cleanup.
+
+## Descriptor registry
+
+Responsibilities:
+
+- ingest descriptors only from authenticated approved worker builds or immutable
+  release artifacts tied to those builds;
+- validate the worker-owned descriptor contract, size bounds, and digest;
+- retain immutable descriptor bytes;
+- expose descriptors to UI and desired-state validation;
+- record approval policy and worker-group availability;
+- reject conflicting bytes for the same digest or identity.
+
+It does not author provider fields or capabilities.
+
+## Client desired-state service
+
+Responsibilities:
+
+- create and update client specifications with revision CAS;
+- require an approved exact descriptor digest;
+- separate public and secret fields according to that descriptor;
+- perform structural validation;
+- create tombstones for disable and delete operations;
+- write audit records;
+- enqueue reconciliation.
+
+Saving desired state never calls a provider API.
+
+## Secret service
+
+Responsibilities:
+
+- encrypt secret versions at rest or store external vault references;
+- never return an existing secret value through ordinary GET/list APIs;
+- accept only names declared secret by the exact descriptor;
+- seal assigned secrets to an authenticated worker identity;
+- rotate and revoke versions;
+- accept bounded version-checked secret replacement when a runtime plugin rotates
+  a credential;
+- prevent logs, errors, and audit summaries from containing secret material.
+
+A secret patch uses compare-and-swap:
+
+```text
+client_id
+secret_name
+expected_secret_version
+replacement encrypted value
+worker_id / worker_incarnation
+active runtime generation evidence
+```
+
+A conflict never overwrites a newer secret.
+
+## Route and namespace service
+
+Responsibilities:
+
+- map bucket, host, path, or prefix to a client and implementation-relative root;
+- bind policy and credential sets;
+- verify desired dependencies;
+- compile protocol-specific desired configuration;
+- ensure route withdrawal precedes destructive client closure.
+
+It does not choose a live socket, provider connection, or runtime generation.
+
+## Worker registry
+
+A worker registers with authenticated workload identity and reports:
+
+```text
+worker incarnation
+implementation language
+compiled lanes
+control carriers
+build digest
+capability manifest digest
+plugin IDs, versions, contract versions, and descriptor digests
+```
+
+The registry rejects stale incarnations and unsupported contract versions.
+
+## Assignment compiler
+
+The compiler assigns a resource only when the worker manifest contains:
+
+- the required lane;
+- the required protocol/policy contract;
+- the exact client plugin ID and contract version;
+- the selected implementation ID;
+- the exact approved descriptor digest;
+- any other declared capabilities required by the route.
+
+The compiler does not know capability from a Go build tag. It trusts the signed
+worker capability manifest and approved build digest.
+
+There is no implicit fallback from one plugin or implementation to another.
+Fallback is explicit desired route/policy state.
+
+## Snapshot compiler
+
+The compiler converts persistent desired state into the worker-owned wire
+contract. Each plugin resource references an exact descriptor digest.
+
+Conceptual form:
+
+```json
+{
+  "schema": "network-data-plane-worker/desired/v1",
+  "snapshot_revision": 42,
+  "worker_group": "storage-east",
+  "resources": [
+    {
+      "id": "client-7",
+      "contract": "network-data-plane-worker/client/v1",
+      "plugin_id": "selected.plugin",
+      "plugin_version": "1.2.3",
+      "implementation_id": "selected-adapter",
+      "descriptor_digest": "sha256:...",
+      "config_revision": 9,
+      "public_config": {},
+      "secret_lease_refs": []
+    }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+The control plane does not embed object bodies, provider access tokens in clear
+text, executable plugin code, live sockets, or connection handles.
+
+## Reconciler
+
+The reconciler compares desired snapshots with observed worker state.
+
+It may:
+
+- retry snapshot delivery;
+- pause or roll back a rollout;
+- reassign resources when a worker disappears;
+- report descriptor/config/capability mismatch;
+- wait for old-generation drain;
+- mark degraded status.
+
+It must not assume a timed-out control request physically stopped plugin work.
+The worker's observed generation and drain state are authoritative.
+
+# Runtime rollout
+
+## Prepare and publish
+
+```text
+control plane commits desired revision
+    -> compiles signed snapshot referencing exact descriptor digest
+worker verifies snapshot and capability manifest
+    -> resolves public config and sealed secrets
+    -> plugin prepares and validates candidate
+    -> worker publishes candidate generation
+    -> worker ACKs applied state
+    -> old generation drains
+    -> worker reports drained or cleanup failure
+```
+
+Candidate failure preserves the current generation.
+
+## Destructive change ordering
+
+For disable or delete:
+
+```text
+withdraw routes that admit new work
+wait for route publication ACK
+request client removal
+worker drains pinned exchanges
+worker closes client generation
+control plane records absent/drained state
+retain tombstone and audit history
+```
+
+Deleting a database row before route withdrawal and runtime drain is forbidden.
+
+## Control-plane outage
+
+Workers continue using the last-known-good applied snapshot. They do not require a
+per-request lease renewal from the control plane. Expiring secrets or explicit
+operator policy may eventually close admission, but ordinary control-plane
+unavailability alone does not interrupt an admitted transfer.
+
+# Security requirements
+
+## Worker identity
+
+Registration and secret delivery require authenticated workload identity. A
+worker ID string or network location is not sufficient.
+
+## Descriptor integrity
+
+- descriptors are bound to approved build and plugin digests;
+- cached bytes are immutable by digest;
+- descriptor labels and help text are escaped before display;
+- an unapproved worker cannot redefine a trusted plugin identity;
+- assignment references the exact descriptor digest.
+
+## Secret handling
+
+- public and secret fields are separated according to the worker descriptor;
+- secret values are write-only in ordinary management APIs;
+- desired snapshots contain sealed leases or references, not plaintext;
+- observed state and audit records contain sanitized codes, not provider errors
+  with credentials;
+- plugin-requested secret names are checked against the pinned descriptor.
+
+## Management authorization
+
+Administrative APIs use explicit RBAC. Worker control endpoints use workload
+identity and revision/incarnation fencing. Loopback or UDS reachability alone is
+not identity.
+
+## Artifact policy
+
+Control-plane approval of a build or descriptor does not sandbox native code.
+Native plugins run with worker process authority unless a separately enforced
+sandbox exists. Strict isolation may require separate worker deployments.
+
+# Product plugin implementation migration
+
+OpenList driver packages are valuable implementation sources. They should be
+adapted into a concrete worker client plugin without moving OpenList server,
+mount, UI, database, or protocol semantics into the plugin.
+
+That implementation must:
+
+- import the worker-owned plugin and Client contracts;
+- publish its concrete descriptor through the worker contract;
+- translate selected driver configuration into its descriptor fields;
+- identify secret fields explicitly;
+- expose only selected reusable driver classes;
+- preserve provider-specific authentication, object IDs, links, uploads, and
+  authoritative results;
+- avoid exposing OpenList `model.Obj`, mount paths, `internal/fs`, router, or
+  storage database types to the generic worker core.
+
+Composite drivers that depend on OpenList mounts, hooks, or hidden database state
+are not automatically valid independent client implementations. Their dependency
+model must become explicit or they remain unsupported in the first plugin build.
+
+Other concrete client plugins follow the same worker contract. OpenList control
+plane treats their IDs and descriptors as opaque approved runtime capabilities.
+It does not own their backend inventory or field schema.
+
+# Legacy compatibility and migration
+
+## Phase 0: characterize current behavior
+
+- freeze storage CRUD and provider initialization tests;
+- inventory legacy data routes and background tasks;
+- classify current drivers by standalone reusability;
+- record secret-bearing `Addition` fields;
+- record stable storage-ID and mount-path mappings.
+
+## Phase 1: land worker contract dependency
+
+- adopt the worker-owned descriptor and desired-state contracts;
+- stop creating an OpenList-local competing plugin schema;
+- add descriptor cache and approval tables;
+- add worker build registration and capability ingestion.
+
+## Phase 2: split desired and observed state
+
+- add client, secret, route, worker, assignment, snapshot, and observed-state
+  records;
+- keep legacy `Storage` rows unchanged during initial backfill;
+- make desired/observed status visible separately.
+
+## Phase 3: build the OpenList-derived client plugin
+
+- create one compatibility boundary over selected drivers;
+- publish the descriptor from the plugin implementation;
+- implement worker Client operations and lifecycle;
+- add conformance tests for each selected driver class;
+- keep current OpenList server behavior unchanged for legacy traffic.
+
+## Phase 4: dual-write management APIs
+
+- translate legacy storage create/update into new client, secret, and route
+  desired state;
+- retain old API responses for compatibility;
+- compare worker descriptor validation with legacy reflection-derived forms;
+- stop treating legacy runtime status as desired state.
+
+## Phase 5: introduce worker assignments
+
+- register selected worker builds;
+- approve descriptor digests;
+- compile signed snapshots;
+- reconcile prepare/publish/drain status;
+- keep the legacy process available as rollback.
+
+## Phase 6: migrate data routes
+
+Move one profile at a time:
+
+```text
+read-only HTTP/download
+WebDAV
+S3 reads
+single PUT
+multipart and mutations
+remaining selected protocols
+```
+
+Each route cutover includes canary, metrics, rollback, and old-generation drain.
+
+## Phase 7: control-plane-only build
+
+Remove from the control-plane profile:
+
+```text
+ordinary file read/write APIs
+download/proxy endpoints
+WebDAV and S3 data listeners
+FTP/SFTP data services
+provider upload/download task execution
+live provider sessions and mount map
+```
+
+Retain only administration, identity, descriptor registry, desired state, secret
+management, worker control, reconciliation, status, and audit.
+
+## Phase 8: retire legacy storage execution
+
+- stop initializing drivers in control-plane storage CRUD;
+- remove process-global mount execution from the control-plane binary;
+- migrate remaining legacy records or explicitly quarantine unsupported drivers;
+- delete compatibility code only after rollback windows close.
+
+# Validation
+
+## Contract validation
+
+- OpenList accepts only worker contract versions it supports;
+- descriptor digest mismatch fails closed;
+- cached descriptors cannot be edited in place;
+- secret fields are taken from the exact worker descriptor;
+- control-plane forms do not initialize live clients;
+- final worker/plugin validation can reject structurally valid desired state;
+- control-plane status distinguishes desired, assigned, applied, and drained.
+
+## Reconciliation validation
+
+- failed candidate keeps the old generation active;
+- stale worker incarnation and revision updates are rejected;
+- route withdrawal precedes client removal;
+- control timeout is not treated as physical cleanup;
+- worker disappearance triggers explicit reassignment policy;
+- last-known-good workers continue serving during control-plane outage.
+
+## Migration validation
+
+- every legacy storage ID has deterministic client/route mapping;
+- secret-bearing `Addition` values are encrypted or externally referenced;
+- mount-path behavior is represented by routes, not client identity;
+- unsupported composite drivers are reported, not silently flattened;
+- legacy and new status values are not conflated.
+
+# Non-goals
+
+This specification does not create:
+
+- a control-plane-owned plugin contract or schema;
+- per-request provider RPC through the control plane;
+- object-byte proxying through the control plane;
+- dynamic code installation from desired state;
+- automatic fallback between client implementations;
+- a guarantee that every legacy driver becomes an independent plugin adapter;
+- full object-store semantics for cloud drives;
+- proof that native plugin code is sandboxed.
+
+# Definition of done
+
+- OpenList serves as `openlist-control-plane` for desired state and reconciliation.
+- The worker specification is the canonical owner of plugin descriptor, schema,
+  secret, lifecycle, capability, and execution contracts.
+- OpenList caches and approves exact worker-published descriptors without
+  redefining them.
+- Public and secret configuration are separated by the pinned descriptor.
+- Assignments reference exact worker build and descriptor digests.
+- Object bytes and provider sessions remain in data-plane workers.
+- Legacy `Storage` rows are split into client, secret, route, and observed state.
+- Provider initialization no longer occurs in control-plane CRUD.
+- Replacement is prepare-publish-drain and preserves the old generation on
+  failure.
+- The final control-plane build contains no ordinary file data path.
+- CI covers contract ingestion, desired-state validation, reconciliation,
+  migration, security, and outage behavior.
